#!ipxe
# Embedded in every loader rescriptum ships, and this is the entry point of the whole
# chain. It answers the one question DHCP cannot: how does iPXE end up talking to *us*
# rather than to itself or to the internet?
#
#   - A plain undionly.kpxe from ipxe.org does DHCP, is told to load iPXE, and loads
#     itself forever. That is iPXE's documented chainloading loop.
#   - A stock netboot.xyz binary chains to the public boot.netboot.xyz. No loop, but our
#     menu and our answers are never consulted.
#
# The indirection through ${next-server} is ours rather than upstream's — its own
# chainloading example names a fixed URL — and it is what lets one generic build serve
# every deployment: option 66 is how this loader arrived in the first place.
#
# The port is a contract. This script can read no configuration; it is baked in before
# any deployment exists, so 8001 is as fixed here as an answer URL baked into an ISO.
# Moving RESCRIPTUM_MEDIA_ADDR is allowed and survivable — see the guide — but every
# loader already shipped assumes this.
dhcp
isset ${next-server} && chain http://${next-server}:8001/ipxe/bootstrap || chain ../ipxe/bootstrap
